<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco Combes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1620">1620</a>–<a href="/years/1665">1665</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco Combes was a Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. He worked for years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and wrote the Historia de las islas de Mindanao, Iolo y sus adyacentes, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1667. The book is a major source for the Muslim sultanates and the peoples of Mindanao in the 17th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Combes was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1620. At twelve he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> order as a novice at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a>, in Catalonia, and after six years of study, before he had finished his training for the priesthood, he asked to join the mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. He was sent first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where he joined a group of Catholic missionaries bound for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>, and in 1643 he reached the Philippines with forty-six others in an expedition led by Diego de Bobadilla. He completed his theological studies there and was ordained in 1645.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was posted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamboanga-city">Zamboanga</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>, where for twelve years he ministered to the local people and gained standing as a leader, serving as envoy to the Muslim chieftains, among them Sultan Corralat and the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a>. He spent two years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> from 1657 and then three on Leyte, where he studied the island's history, customs and language. Recalled to Manila in 1662, he sensed that the Spanish hold on the Muslim areas of Mindanao was weakening and urged the government and the church authorities to keep and strengthen the forts there. He died on board ship on 29 December 1665, aged 45, while sailing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as procurator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His Historia was printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> by the Herederos de Pablo de Val two years after his death. It includes a three-paragraph account of the voyage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a> in Philippine waters in March and April 1521, in which the fleet anchors at Butuan and plants a cross on a mountain there before sailing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> with a brief stop at an island called Limasaua. That account is now read differently: the fleet is held to have anchored at Limasaua, today spelled Limasawa, where a mass that Combes does not mention was held. It is commemorated every year in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> as the first mass in the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francisco Combés” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dapitan is known from Spanish accounts, especially the Jesuit historian <b>Francisco Combes</b>, and from Boholano tradition.</blockquote><a href="/state/kedatuan-of-dapitan">Kedatuan of Dapitan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco de Juárez</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.65,-0.88&amp;year=1649" aria-label="Zaragoza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Francisco Combes"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4944 4010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4944 4010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.65,-0.88&amp;year=1649">What was here in 1649? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
